    "q-161175": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161175",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $y=3x+2z$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 16,\\,x\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=3x+2z,\\,(x,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 16,\\,x\\le 0,\\,z\\ge 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14119930/assets/48ffe2b0bd40937205d9b9d3b6cc264f83bd56ea30a5df318ba7c7507efb68f7.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+2z)=3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3x+2z)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9+4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{14}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
